On Tue, 15 Nov 1994, Harry S. Hawk wrote:

> This doesn't surprise me. Are you running unix? I thinkn you
> only need scsi if you are going to run Unix....

Not true - SCSI runs on all platforms, including apples.  (Whence it came,
actually.)

> Many motherboards have IDE built in. SCSI drives cost more then IDE
> drives.

Many do but not typically on your higher end systems.  Those that do have
IDE on the motherboard also let you disable it.

SCSI drives on larger sizes run about the same as IDE.  For example, you
can get a 2 GB drive including external enclosure for about $850.  IDE you
pay a little less for 1GB (haven't seen any 2GB IDE yet.)

SCSI-II, fast SCSI, wide SCSI, all offer faster drive throughput and
latency compared to IDE drives.  If disk throughput is a concern, I'd go SCSI.

Also, your better CD-ROM drives, scanners, plotters use SCSI.

And while SCSI controllers cost a little more, they also allow you to have
up to 7 disk drives/SCSI devices per controller card.

My favorite on an ISA bus is the Adaptek 1542CF.  It is internally
supported by DOS, Win NT, Unixware, SCO Unix, OS/2, and Netware.  Makes it
easy to switch between operating systems.  And it goes for approx. $220.

So if you plan on having a CD-ROM, scanner or plotter you might want to go
SCSI from the start and eliminate having two different drive controllers
on your system.

-NetSurfer

