CLOSING THE "VALUES-GAP": 
Democracy Without Democrats
By Vigdor Schreibman 

	As this year began, I described how the most sacred love of my
country--the United States of America--had somehow entered my psyche under
the abominable conditions of the great depression; enough to fill my whole
life with purpose [Fins-NC2-03].  I wrote, "This sacred country that is the
blueprint for my deepest cognitive reality is comprised of the democratic
mission of Thomas Jefferson, the humanitarian spirit of the Declaration of
Independence ... and the promise of equal justice under law."  I also noted
the contradictions in our paramount reality: "While we are committed in our
deepest beliefs to sustain and enhance democracy we are governed by a set of
decadent ideologies--by opportunism, materialism, individualism and the
morality of the marketplace--that are bereft of human, social, and ecological
enlightenment but the causes of raging anger and frustration."

	Those value conflicts have been fully documented by conservative pundit
Kevin Phillips [Arrogant Capital (1994)]. Citizens are also confounded by the
settled belief that the American political system has been subjected to a
"hostile takeover" [Kettering Foundation, 1991]. "In the end," writes David
Mathews, head of Kettering, "citizens believe that they do not--cannot--have
a say in this system."  The "information superhighways" has exacerbated these
conditions by enlarging the instrumental power of government officials and
private industry, in a transformation that will radically disarm popular
government despite promises to support democracy.  Finally, exploding at the
ballot box Nov. 8th, voters expressed their outrage over the inequitable
political system by demolishing the Democratic power structure in Congress.
Only 12 percent of voters support the Republican program; most voted in
protest against the politics of rich and poor played by Democrats, which is
seen as a betrayal of President Clinton's pledge to reverse this pattern of
behavior, as disclosed in the Nov. 21, 1994 issue of TIME Magazine. 

	The situation gives one pause for reflection.  A friendly fascist right
wing has absorbed the Republican Party of Abraham Lincoln, led by Newt
Gingrich, and supported by middle-class white Southern conservatives and an
illicit, coordinated attack strategy of the conservative Christian Coalition.
Meanwhile, the Democratic Party of Thomas Jefferson has adopted the
Republican rule of "artificial aristocrats," asserting arbitrary governance
by strategically placed persons, also sustained by the collapse of citizen
responsibility for self-governance.  Gingrich talks about a "Contract with
America" that would only intensify the politics of rich and poor, revert to
an ethic of militarism, and repudiate the "Social Contract" that is our
founding creed.  The United States has become a Democracy without Democrats. 

	Nevertheless, the most powerful idea that still resonates throughout
this society--that the free and informed exercise of the power of the people
to control their own destiny--is the sine qua non of republican governance. 
This idea is sustained by the constitutional assumption affirmed by the
Supreme Court that "All power is derived from the people."  

	We are now undergoing two global revolutions, involving the environment
and electronic information networks, both of which will radically change all
our human, social, and ecological conditions.  The President's Council on
Sustainable Development has already suggested a holistic vision for that
possibility, of "a life-sustaining earth," together with a set of draft
principles and action strategy, now being further revised [Fins-SD-07]. The
United Nations International Telecommunications Union, in furtherance of the
development of a Global Information Infrastructure proposed by Vice President
Al Gore, invited study groups Sept 23, to consider a comprehensive set of
question raised by this anticipated transformation [Fins-SD-08].  Citizens
will either plan their future together, or someone will dictate it to them.

	It is a bedeviling situation in which we find ourselves, in the midst
of a domestic rebellion and two global revolutions, with just two years to
turn things around by rediscovering our roots and transforming our political
leadership, consistent with the democratic mission.  During this period the
vital work of the U.S. Congress will most likely be lost to a grim deadlock
and cynical process of self-destruction, led by Newt Gingrich and his gang.
We must confront them directly, and unequivocally hold the democratic line,
prepared for the worst that they will likely offer.  Meanwhile, the so-called
"new Democrats" in the White House will be searching for new insight, either
recognizing what went wrong and how to fix it, or going down badly in shame.

	The time has come for a good talk--just between voters without the power
brokers--to freely and fairly define what we desire for our shared future and
how best to get it.  This dialogue should be carried out in preparation for
the Presidential election of 1996, to inform the electorate and plan for a
new, authoritative "Social Contract" in America, and a government committed
to implementing it.  A multibillion dollar infrastructure can be established
for that purpose by tapping appropriated funds for information technology,
to arm popular government in the Information Age and assure a life-sustaining
earth.  The process could be organized under a Presidential/State Council in
collaboration with schools and libraries.  Voters could deliberate face-to-
face and electronically, building a consensus for the future derived from
the paramount power of the people to control their own destiny in our time. 

