> as message #517 from some other month would be included.
>
>     Alternatively, let us assume that the user wasn't sure if "recursive
> programming techniques" was really that interesting, and decided to
> request the above message using a '::resend' command. Upon receiving it,
> and concluding that in fact they did want to receive all future messages
> on that subject, they could simply reply to the message requested with the
> command:
>
>     ::include thread
>
>     Recall that, without arguments, MailWeir will automatically derive the
> subject from the message being replied to.
>
>     As another example of the '::include' command, assume a different user
> is receiving most messages from the list, but has previously filtered out
> all messages on the "recursive" subject above. Upon reviewing the nightly
> filter list, they see that Joe Shmoe is posting messages on this subject,
> and Joe Shmoe just happens to be an old college buddy and someone worth
> listening to. So, the user requests one of Joe's posts (message #95-3-71)
> on the subject of recursive programming techniques using the '::resend'
> command. After reading it, our hypothetical user decides that, while the
> subject may be a bit boring, Joe is such an excellent writer that anything
> he writes is worth listening to. So, the user sends a reply to Joe's post
> with any of the following command formats:
>
>     ::include user
>     ::include user jshmoe@vax.companyx.com
>     ::include user #71
>     ::include user #95-3-71
>
>     Note that, as with a previous example, if this wasn't the month of
> march, 1995, the short-hand message# format would not work.
>
>     To override all previous filter conditions:
>
>     ::include all
>
